    "message": {
     "role": "assistant",
     "content": "We spent 3 months building a feature nobody used.\n\nIt’s still the best lesson of my career. 👇\n\nTwo years ago, my team was convinced our users wanted advanced custom dashboards.\n\nSales kept asking for it. Competitors had it. It sounded impressive on a roadmap.\n\nSo we built it. Filters, widgets, drag-and-drop, exports — the whole thing. We polished every pixel.\n\nLaunch day came. We announced it with pride.\n\n...crickets.\n\nAfter 30 days, exactly 47 people had tried it. After 60 days, only 3 were still using it. We’d built a Ferrari for people who just wanted a faster bike.\n\nIt hurt. But it taught me something I’ll never forget:\n\n**Fall in love with the problem, not your solution.**\n\nWe hadn’t solved a real, painful, frequent problem. We’d built what *we* thought was cool, without watching a single user struggle first.\n\nNow I do 3 things differently before I ship anything:\n\n**1. Talk first, build second.** 5 customer conversations > 50 internal opinions.\n**2. Ship the smallest test.** Could we have validated this with a fake door test or a simple CSV export?\n**3. Define success + kill criteria upfront.** If X% don’t use it in Y days, we kill it. No ego.\n\nThat flop made me a better builder. I now get more joy from deleting unused code than shipping new code.\n\nHave you ever shipped something nobody used?\n\nWhat did it teach you?\n\n#ProductManagement #Startups #Leadership #LessonsLearned",
